{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>My sin is too great to bear</b> — in bewilderment, you bring charges against those above and those below, and my sin cannot be contested: this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 4:13) from Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 22:11). But the correct sense in the plain reading is that it is a confession. He said the truth: my sin is too great to be forgiven, yet You are righteous, YHWH, and upright are Your judgments — even though You have punished me very greatly, and behold You have driven me today from the face of the earth, for when I am a fugitive and a wanderer and cannot remain in one place, behold I am banished from the earth and there is no place for my rest; and from Your presence I will hide, for I cannot stand before You to pray or to offer a sacrifice and a meal-offering, for I am ashamed and also confounded, for I bear the reproach of my youth. But what shall I do, for whoever finds me will kill me, and You in Your abundant kindness did not impose death upon me. And the meaning of what he said before Him is: behold, my sin is great, and You have increased my punishment; but guard me lest I be punished more than what You have imposed upon me, for because I will be a fugitive and a wanderer and will not build for myself a house or fences in any place, the beasts will kill me, for Your shade has departed from me. He acknowledged that man is not exalted and delivered by his own strength, but only through the guarding of the Most High upon him."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:24:38.057771892Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}